d then they are forking your knight  here on e4  looking to get an easy piece  now unfortunately for white this is not  going to work out as they had  hoped and really i think they're  expecting black to play maybe knight to  c6  and then queen captures here on e4 no  real good options for black to continue  or  same thing with bishop to d7 both of  those are going to end with  queen takes here on e4 the main thing  that white has to be careful of is the  move  knight to d7 it looks somewhat odd  because  all this does is it blocks the attack on  the king  but it also blocks the queen here on d8  and also blocks  the bishop here on c8 but this is the  trap  because after the queen takes here on e4  then the move knight to c5  is deadly because this knight is now  attacking the queen here on e4  and it's also attacking this square here  on d3 anytime your opponent plays their  knight here to e2 it's smothering their  own  king and so you can start to look for  opportunities to get your knight  involved into the game and  really attack these squares if they're  not protected by a pawn so the move you  can see here knight 2 d3  is very very powerful the square is  defended by the queen now here on  d8 so how does white continue well kind  of two moves here  we'll look at both e3 and d4 here the  first one is  e3 you can still see exactly what we  were talking about here and that's  the knight coming here to d3 check  king comes to d1 now the pawn  takes on b2 uh they have two options  one that they can go ahead and take with  their bishop they could move their rook  over here to a b1  if they re move their rook then pawn  takes here on c1  capturing that bishop promoting to a  queen the knight takes because  no reason to give up your rook here on  c1 so we take with our knight right here  and then after they take you can see  we're now up in  material uh as far as the pawn advantage  we also have  the bishop pair so this is going to be  good for black and also  their king is just in a really tough  spot can't castle um  it's out in the center of the board so  black definitely should have a good game  here if they capture with  their bishop that's fine as well we can  now take with our  knight check and after king 2e1 we can  just  even come back here to a d3  check maybe king d1 and then bishop to  g4 getting more material involved into  the game so let's come back maybe they  don't play queen to e3  what if they play instead queen to  d4 okay well here we can just play  knight 2 d3 as we talked about before  they go ahead and take here that they  can't just take our knight because then  we're just gonna take their queen that's  definitely not a good exchange for  for white so they play king 2 d1 now we  can go ahead and take their queen they  take  our queen but then we have the move  knight to f2  check we're now forking this rook here  on h1 so after they move the king  we played knight takes on h1 and you can  now see  we are up in material uh not only up in  pawns but also up in the fact that we  traded off  uh a knight for a rook so  uh definitely going to be a good  position from black standpoint so  instead of taking  early on you know we said that the queen  taking here on e4  is a mistake really what white should be  doing in this spot  is the move knight takes here on c3  if they do play knight takes here on c3  we're going to take our  knight here from e4 and we're going to  move it to  c5 this attacks the queen here on  a4 and after the queen moves back you  know it really could move back  anywhere then we're going to play the  move pawn to  c6 and this really stops a lot of the  threats that white has of bringing the  knight down here to  d5 or b5 it also opens up for the queen  to get involved  into the game you definitely don't want  to make a mistake and play you know  let's  you know knight takes here on e5 opens  up for the queen to get involved into  the game because then they could just  play  pawn to d4 and now they're forking both  of our knights so uh early on  if we look at this you know white really  had what almost seem like a trap of yeah  go ahead and take this pawn here because  then i can get the queen involved into  the game  but after we play the move knight to d7  which looks like it blocks off things  for black  uh this is actually the trap hoping that  they take  the knight here on e4 and then we just  play the move knight to  c5 but remember even if they don't if  they take here on  c3 we're still in a good spot we played  knight to c5  forcing the queen back and then we can  just continue with our game plan  pawn to c6 then we can play pawn to e6  at some point get our dark square  mission bomb to the game castle on  the king side is really the way that  we're going to continue so that is the  sleaze meister trap  always be wary of what your opponent's  going to do maybe sometimes you think  you're laying a trap but actually  they are hoping that you take specific  material just see all the threats they  have on board  especially if they have uh ways that  they can  get their queen involved into the game  and also if your king  looks like it is trapped in any capacity  that's definitely a red flag that you  have to pay attention to so hopefully  you guys enjoyed  this trap if you have other videos you  want me to make please let me know but  thank you guys so much for watching i'll  see you in the next video

